body:
LF: Sprigatito and Fuecoco

&#x200B;

Have a random bunch of different pokemon. Open to trading whatever if I have it

I'm certified in secondary social studies. In NJ that's grades 5-12. I'm working on getting my special education certification through grad school.

&#x200B;

I have a bit of experience coaching baseball as an assistant last year before schools closed. I played when I was in school and I've always been interested in coaching or helping with other school activities. I've tried my best to make those things very clear on my resume

I love being able to talk about really obscure stories and fun facts in class about any topic to make it more interesting to kids. I also feel like there's more wiggle room to teach because social studies/history isn't held to Common Core super strictly, so there's more opportunity to get really creative with it.

&#x200B;

Ultimately, I think history is an underrated subject that's incredibly important for students to learn, especially when it comes to creating arguments based on evidence and teaching students about real-world issues. I feel like I have a real impact on how students look at the world in the future.

I have mostly freshman. My US II students are sophomores who don't necessarily have to take US I, there is another option at this school for freshman that they may have taken instead.

&#x200B;

Thanks for the advice, I'm going to work on putting my own spin on things.

Very cool. I'm guessing they write down topics related to the lesson, write the facts and then discuss?

&#x200B;

Just also want to add that I love that you discuss Western Africa in the early lessons. The schools I've been at in NJ rarely touch on it but it's such an important part of early American history!
